notes               = "One poem running across two printed pages: page 169 carries no date and the poem finishes on 170. Strongly rhymed and half-metrical (هجرانی / بارانی / حیرانی / بیابانی، دارد / می‌بارد، برگی / مرگی) — another of the formal poems clustering after fire-water. بی‌داد in the last line of 169 is printed with the space, so the series is بی‌صدا / بی‌همهمه / بی‌داد — soundless, murmurless, cryless — not بیداد (tyranny). فرهاد is Farhad, the stonecutter of Khosrow and Shirin who cut a road through Mount Bisotun for love; تیشه is his adze. Shahrivar is month 6 → +621 = 1996. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

ای منِ محزون...
دوستانِ عاشقِ سرگشتهٔ تو
مثلِ دریایی...
بعدِ رفتن... ابرشان بر جای می‌ماند...
ابر... دریای مسافر... بحرِ هجرانی...
روح پر شورِ... کویرِ... مستِ... بارانی...
پاکِ... غمناکِ... غریبِ... دشتِ حیرانی...
دودِ آه آهوان...
این تشنه کامانِ بیابانی.
دوستانِ عاشقِ من بعدِ رفتن...
مثلِ دریا... ابرشان برجای می‌ماند....
ابر عاشق...
هر کجا که پاک و غمناک است می‌بارد...
ابر جانی تازه و روحی کهن دارد
ابر هر جا... ابر هر دَم... قسمت خاک است می‌بارد.
ابر با جادوی غم... در جانِ جنگل جای می‌گیرد...
و قناری پای هر برگی...
بی‌خبر از هیبت بی‌سایهٔ مرگی...
تا نفس باقی‌ست...
آخرین خطِ کجِ سیم قفس باقی‌ست می‌خواند...
من ندانستم...
شاید این سرگشته عاشق هم نمی‌داند...
بی‌صدا... بی‌همهمه... بی‌داد...
با قلم... با تیشه و اندیشهٔ فرهاد...
عشق در جانِ جهان...
جاوید می‌ماند...
شهریور ۱۳۷۵

===machine===

O my sorrowing self...
Your wandering friends, the lovers,
are like a sea...
after the going... their cloud stays behind...
Cloud... the travelling sea... the ocean of separation...
the ardent soul... of the desert... drunk... rain-heavy...
pure... grieving... a stranger... the plain of bewilderment...
the smoke of the gazelles' sighing...
these parched-mouthed ones of the wilderness.
My friends, the lovers, after the going...
like the sea... their cloud stays behind....
The cloud in love...
rains wherever it is pure and grieving...
The cloud has a fresh life and an ancient soul.
The cloud everywhere... the cloud every moment... it is the earth's portion, it rains.
The cloud, with the magic of grief... settles into the soul of the forest...
and a canary, at the foot of every leaf...
knowing nothing of the shadowless dread of a death...
sings while the breath is left...
while the last crooked line of the cage-wire is left...
I did not know...
Perhaps this wandering lover does not know either...
Soundless... without a murmur... without a cry...
With the pen... with the adze and the mind of Farhad...
love, in the soul of the world...
stays for ever...
Shahrivar 1375

===lantern===

O my sorrowing self —
your wandering friends, the lovers,
are like a sea:
after the going, their cloud stays behind.
Cloud — the travelling sea, the ocean of separation,
the ardent soul of the desert, drunk, rain-heavy,
pure, grieving, a stranger, the plain of bewilderment,
the smoke of the gazelles' sighing,
these parched-mouthed ones of the wilderness.
My friends, the lovers, after the going,
like the sea, their cloud stays behind.
The cloud in love
rains wherever it is pure and grieving.
The cloud has a fresh life and an ancient soul.
The cloud everywhere, the cloud every moment — it is the earth's portion, and it rains.
The cloud, with the magic of grief, settles into the soul of the forest,
and a canary at the foot of every leaf,
knowing nothing of the shadowless dread of a death,
sings while the breath is left to it,
while the last crooked line of the cage-wire is left.
I did not know.
Perhaps this wandering lover does not know either.
Soundless, without a murmur, without a cry —
with the pen, with the adze and the mind of Farhad,
love, in the soul of the world,
stays for ever.

Shahrivar 1375

===translation===

===footnotes===

Farhad is the stonecutter of *Khosrow and Shirin*, who was set to carve a road through
Mount Bisotun for love of Shirin and did it. The poem's last claim is that the pen and the adze do
the same work, and that this is the only thing that lasts.
